# Installation — `./install.sh` and `./start.sh`

Two scripts, one brain:

| | What it does | When |
|---|---|---|
| `./start.sh` | installs the dependencies then runs the node **from the current tree** | trying it out, development, a container |
| `./install.sh` | copies the tree somewhere durable, lays down a startup service, then calls `start.sh` | **the recommended method** on a machine meant to host a node |

`install.sh` reimplements **nothing** from `start.sh`: it delegates all the
dependency work to it (`NMESH_SETUP_ONLY=1 ./start.sh`), and the service it
writes points at `start.sh`, never at `python` directly. The intended
consequence: a node that restarts re-checks its own installation and repairs
itself after a distro upgrade or a broken venv. Docker is still possible
(`docker/`), but it is no longer the advised route for a dedicated machine.

```bash
./install.sh                        # installs, enables at boot, runs
./install.sh --fleet                # …and enables the fleet app
./install.sh --prefix /srv/nmesh    # choose where it lands
./install.sh --no-start             # installs and enables, without running
./install.sh --uninstall            # removes the service and the files
./install.sh --uninstall --purge    # …and the node's identity and state
```

Any other argument is passed to the node, exactly as with `start.sh`.

### Where it lands

| | Tree | State (identity, certs, sessions) |
|---|---|---|
| root | `/opt/nmesh` | `/var/lib/nmesh` |
| user | `~/.local/share/nmesh` | `~/.local/share/nmesh/data` |

Overrides: `--prefix` / `NMESH_PREFIX`, `--data` / `NMESH_DATA`,
`--service` / `NMESH_SERVICE` (default `nmesh`), `--run-as` / `NMESH_USER`.

The copied tree excludes `.git`, `.venv`, `data/` and the `__pycache__`
directories: neither history, nor the origin machine's virtual environment, nor
another node's state travels.

### The node has an account of its own

The state directory holds the node's **identity key**, its encrypted session
store, its cache of the names it has learned (`node.names`) and the console
password's hash. Running it under the account of whoever
installed it hands all of that to every process of that account — the browser
included.

A **root** installation therefore creates a system account `nmesh` (no login
shell, no password, nothing else on the machine belongs to it) and gives it the
tree **and** the state, both **mode 700**. Nothing but that account and root can
read the identity key.

- `--run-as root`: create no account, the node runs as root.
- `--run-as someone`: use an existing account.
- `NMESH_ACCOUNT=name`: change the name of the account created.
- Account impossible to create (an exotic distro) → a warning, falling back on
  the previous behaviour; the installation does not fail for it.
- A **non-root** installation: no account can be created, everything belongs to
  the current user in 700, and the script says so plainly.
- `--uninstall --purge` removes the account too, since nothing belongs to it any
  more. Without `--purge` it is kept: it owns the state being kept.

The generated systemd unit adds `NoNewPrivileges`, `PrivateTmp`,
`PrivateDevices` and `ProtectSystem=full`.

**The confinement follows the update right**, and that is not a preference:
`NoNewPrivileges=yes` makes the kernel refuse **every setuid binary** for that
process and its children — so `sudo`, so the update wrapper, with a message that
talks about a kernel flag and suggests nothing to do about it. And
`ProtectSystem=full` mounts `/usr` read-only, where a package manager can write
nothing. A node holding the update grant therefore gets a unit relaxed on those
three directives (`PrivateTmp` stays), with a comment in the unit explaining
why.

**The grant is the default**, so that is the unit a plain `./install.sh`
writes. A node that cannot take a security update is a worse outcome than a
node that can, and what is granted is one fixed script and nothing else (below).
`--no-allow-update` is how to say no: it removes the sudoers rule **and**
restores the hardened unit, because the two cannot be chosen separately or one
would silently undo the other.

The symptom and the fix: [`FAQ.md`](../../FAQ.md).

An accepted trade-off: the tree belongs to the node's account and not to root,
so the process can rewrite its own code. That is the price of self-repair at
startup and of updating from the console — two things a root-only tree would
break.

### The installation stands on its own

`HOME` is pinned to the installation directory, for the installation's
`start.sh` **as well as** for the service. The consequence: `liboqs` is built in
`<prefix>/_oqs` instead of the installer's home. That is what lets the node —
running under a system account with no usable home — find its crypto library at
startup and repair itself. The venv is already in `<prefix>/.venv`; the liboqs
build tree is deleted afterwards.

A service inherits no session environment: neither `HOME` nor `USER`. `start.sh`
therefore never dereferences a bare `$HOME` any more — under `set -u` that
killed the script at startup (*"HOME: unbound variable"*) and the node went into
a restart loop. `node_home()` resolves a genuinely writable directory: the
environment if it is usable, otherwise the account's passwd entry, otherwise the
installation directory. In practice: a node whose unit is old (with no
`Environment=HOME=`) still starts.

### liboqs is compiled once per machine

Compiling liboqs takes several minutes and produces the same library every time.
It is therefore **cached**, indexed by the version of the `liboqs-python`
wrapper:

| | Cache |
|---|---|
| root | `/var/cache/nmesh/liboqs-<version>` |
| user | `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/nmesh/liboqs-<version>` |

A new installation location (a different `--prefix`, moving from a user install
to `/opt`, a second node on the same machine) **reuses** that cache instead of
recompiling. `start.sh` also looks at the prefixes `install.sh` points it at
(`OQS_REUSE_FROM`, a `:`-separated list): the installer's home, their old user
installation, `/opt/nmesh`.

Reuse is never decided on a version number or a file name: the wrapper actually
**loads** the candidate library and both required algorithms answer, or we
recompile. After the copy, the check is run again **at the destination**. A
wrapper version bump changes the cache key and therefore forces a real rebuild —
wrapper and library must stay paired.

Filling the cache is *best effort*: a read-only or full `/var/cache` costs a
rebuild at the next install, never a failure of this one.

Settings: `NMESH_LIBOQS_CACHE=path` (where the cache lives),
`OQS_REUSE_FROM=a:b:c` (prefixes to examine), `OQS_INSTALL_PATH` (the
destination).

Updating the node (from the console or by reinstalling) does not touch `_oqs`:
nothing is recompiled.

### The configuration file

Everything the launcher accepts as an option lives in **`<prefix>/nmesh.conf`**
(by default `/opt/nmesh/nmesh.conf`). That is the file `install.sh` writes and
the console edits: the options of a node installed as a service are no longer
buried in a unit nobody wants to open.

Precedence: **command line > file > default**. An explicitly passed flag always
wins, so an existing unit that passes arguments keeps behaving exactly as
before.

```
# Enable the fleet app (remote management, can open a shell)
fleet = true
console_port = 8787
listen = 0.0.0.0:9000

# Display name for this node, shown beside its id (at most 50 characters)
pseudo = Alice Ada
```

`pseudo` is validated exactly as the network validates it, so a name the mesh
would reject is reported here instead of leaving a node running unnamed — see
[`Docs/Pseudos/guide`](../Pseudos/guide). It is also editable live from the
console (Settings → Identity), which writes it back to this file.

- `./install.sh --fleet --console-host 0.0.0.0` writes those settings into the
  file instead of putting them in `ExecStart`; what the file cannot express
  stays on the command line, nothing is silently lost. The merge is done by the
  node's own code (`scripts/nmesh_config.py`), not reimplemented in shell: one
  parser, one validator.
- Reinstalling does not overwrite it: only the options actually given again
  change.
- It survives updates (the updater replaces only code and documentation) and a
  reinstallation (`copy_tree` erases nothing).
- The location can be overridden: `--config path` or `NMESH_CONFIG`.
- Written **0600**, atomically, like the identity key.

Two settings are deliberately **not** editable from the console:

| | Why |
|---|---|
| `launch` | it chooses what the node **runs**; that belongs to whoever holds the file, not to a web form. The console displays it so nobody is surprised by what is running |
| `data` | the installer decides where the state lives |

The console password is not in there either: that would be a credential in the
clear in a file meant to be read and edited. It stays in
`$NMESH_CONSOLE_PASSWORD`, or is generated and shown once.

Two things the launcher no longer does:

- `start.sh` no longer injects `--udp 9001 --stun` on every run. Since a flag
  beats the file by construction, `udp`, `no_udp` and `stun` could be set in
  `nmesh.conf` and were silently ignored — the file described a node that
  behaved otherwise. Those defaults now live in `src/config.py`, in one place.
  `NMESH_UDP_PORT` is still honoured: asking for a port through the environment
  is an explicit request.
- An accepted consequence: `stun` defaults to **`true`**, because that is what
  every node launched by `start.sh` has always done. Running
  `scripts/nmesh_node.py` directly therefore turns STUN on too, where it used to
  be off — one default, visible and changeable, instead of two that contradicted
  each other.

At startup the node prints the file's problems **and** the settings overridden
by the command line (`Config warn`). A silently ignored file is exactly the failure
you want to see.

Defensive reading, like every input to the process: size and line count bounded,
values validated one by one, unknown keys reported and ignored. **An
incomprehensible file is reported and then set aside, never fatal** — a node
that refuses to start is a worse outcome than a node on its defaults. The
problems appear at startup *and* in the console.

Editing from the console: **Settings → Configuration**. The form is built from
what the node declares, never from a list hard-coded in the page — a setting
added on the node side appears by itself, and a setting the node refuses to
expose cannot be invented from the browser. Nothing is applied live: changes
take effect when the node restarts, and the interface says so.

#### Transport settings (`scheme.option`)

The file also accepts **namespaced** keys, one section per medium:

```
tcp.connect_timeout = 6
tcp.families = ipv4
udp.keepalive_interval = 40
spool.poll_interval = 0.5
```

The file carries them **as text and does not validate them**: the medium is the
only thing that knows what it takes. At startup they are distributed before
anything listens or dials; a refused value or an unknown scheme comes out as
`Config warn` and stays at its default. In the console: **Network → Reachability**,
one panel per transport, rendered from what the transport declares. See
[`Docs/Architecture/transports.md`](../Architecture/transports.md).

### Letting the node run system updates

```bash
./install.sh                         # grants the right (the default)
./install.sh --no-allow-update       # takes it back, and re-hardens the unit
```

The right is granted unless refused. The reasoning is the one that applies to
every other default in this project: the failure it prevents — a node that
cannot take a security update and needs a human on the machine to get one — is
more likely, and worse, than the one it risks. What it risks is bounded to a
single script, which is the point of the next paragraph.

The fleet app's `update` capability needs root **with no human in front**. The
right granted is not a general sudo rule — that would make any bug in the node a
root bug — but **a script** owned by root that the node cannot rewrite, which
refuses every argument and runs a fixed sequence:

```
nmesh ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/lib/nmesh/nmesh-update
```

It sits deliberately under `/usr/local/lib` and not in the installation prefix:
that one belongs to the node's account, which could otherwise rewrite what it is
allowed to run as root. The sudoers file is **validated by `visudo` before
installation** — a file that does not parse can break `sudo` for the whole
machine.

Without that right, the node has no route to root and `update` fails. For a
one-off root command there is the fleet shell: `sudo` there asks a human for
their password, which leaves no permanent access behind.

### Getting back into the console

```bash
./install.sh --reset-password        # a new password, printed once
```

Rewrites the credential in the state directory, gives it back to the node's
account, restarts the service, and **prints the new password once**. It touches
nothing else: not the tree, not the identity, not the certificates. It works on
an already installed node, without reinstalling.

The hashing is done by the node's own code (`scripts/nmesh_password.py`), never
reimplemented in shell: a credential format that diverged between two writers
would be a silent authentication bug. The script reads a chosen password from
**stdin** (`--stdin`) rather than from an argument, where everyone would read it
in `ps`.

Access required: the state directory, that is root or the node's account —
exactly the level of access taking back control should demand.

From the console, with the current password: **Settings → Console password**
(see [`../WebConsole/guide`](../WebConsole/guide)).

### Reinstalling over the top

Running `./install.sh` again **updates in place**: the tree is replaced, **the
state is never touched** (identity, certificates, sessions, venv), the service
is restarted. That is the normal path for a manual update.

### Service managers handled

systemd (system and user), OpenRC, launchd, and **runit** on Android — the
installer offers to add `termux-services` when a phone has no supervisor at all,
because a node that only runs while a terminal is open is the thing `install.sh`
exists to avoid. None of them → the script says so and prints the command to run
by hand; it does not fail for it, and the installation stays valid.

A trap that is handled: `systemctl` present **without** systemd behind it (the
case for many container images) would make every call fail with *"Failed to
connect to bus"*. Detection therefore also requires `/run/systemd/system`, the
real test of a running systemd. Likewise, a service manager that refuses the
service does not fail the installation: it is a warning, and the installed tree
stays runnable by hand.

**Every** unit written exports `NMESH_SERVICE_MANAGED=1`, next to
`Restart=always` (systemd), `KeepAlive` (launchd) or `runsv`'s own restarting
(runit): that pair is what lets a node install an update and then **exit to come
back on the new code**. Replacing the tree does nothing to a process that already
has the old code loaded, so leaving is the update.

The runit service under Termux was the one that did not export it, and the
consequence was invisible in the worst way: `runsv` would have restarted the
node perfectly well, but the node could not tell, so it installed the update and
refused to leave. A phone kept running yesterday's code until somebody reopened
Termux by hand.

Without a supervisor the node no longer gives up either: it **re-execs itself**
(`os.execv`, a fresh interpreter on the same command line, keeping the pid and
the terminal) rather than exiting into nothing. Only when even that is
impossible does it stay up and say to restart it by hand. See
[`../Updates/guide`](../Updates/guide).

The tests source `install.sh` with `NMESH_INSTALL_LIB=1` and exercise init
detection, privileges, paths, account creation, the generated units and the tree
copy without installing anything (`tests/test_install_script.py`).

## Updating from GitHub

A node can check whether a newer release exists and install it — **never without
the operator's explicit confirmation**.

Web console → **Settings → Updates**: *Check for updates* queries GitHub's
releases API; if a newer version exists it is shown with its notes, and
*Install* asks for a confirmation that **names the version**. The install
request repeats that version: if GitHub changed between the display and the
click, the mismatch is refused rather than installing something nobody has seen.

The matching API: `GET /api/update/check`, `POST /api/update/apply` (see
[`../WebConsole/guide`](../WebConsole/guide)).

### Following a branch instead of the releases

`update_branch` in `nmesh.conf` (empty by default; `NMESH_UPDATE_BRANCH`
overrides it, as a flag would) changes **where the answer comes from**, not what
happens next. With a branch set, "is there a newer version?" is answered by the
`__version__` declared in `src/version.py` at that branch —
`raw.githubusercontent.com/<repo>/<branch>/src/version.py`, read with a regular
expression and never executed — and *Install* takes that branch's tree rather
than a tag's. It is for a fleet that tracks development instead of waiting for a
release to be published.

A branch **moves under its own name**, which a tag does not, so the version is
checked twice more: when the install request arrives, and again against the
`src/version.py` inside the downloaded tree. A branch that has moved past the
version the operator confirmed is refused, not installed under the name that was
on screen.

The name is validated where it is stored (`src/config.py`), because it lands in
a URL path: anything that could walk out of the repository's own namespace — a
`..`, a leading slash, a space — is refused rather than escaped, and a value the
file would refuse means "follow the releases" rather than a guess.

What is replaced: **only** the code and documentation directories of the
installed tree. The node's state, the venv and everything else are left intact.
The previous tree is moved to `.nmesh-previous` and restored if the replacement
fails halfway.

Explicit refusals (`updatable()`), rather than breaking an installation:

- the current tree has no `src/`;
- the installation directory is not writable;
- the node runs from a container image → update the image, not the tree.

The trust model, said plainly: this path trusts TLS, GitHub, and whoever can
publish a release in the pinned repository. There is no signature over a GitHub
release that the node verifies.

**That is why the mesh path exists.** A node can also publish its own code as a
package signed with its ML-DSA identity, and a node installs such a release only
from a publisher key its operator pinned by hand — no web host in the trusted
set at all. See [`../Updates/guide`](../Updates/guide). The GitHub route remains
how a node with no peers yet gets its first version.

The current version: `src/version.py` (a test binds that file to
`pyproject.toml`, so the two cannot diverge).

## What `./start.sh` sorts out on its own

`./start.sh` has to start a node on a **fresh** machine, asking for nothing.
Distributions agree on nothing: not on `pip` being present, not on the right to
install anything outside a venv, not on package names. This document lists the
real cases the script handles — if you change one of these behaviours, update
this file in the same commit.

```bash
./start.sh                     # installs everything then runs the node
NMESH_SETUP_ONLY=1 ./start.sh  # installs and verifies, without running the node (CI)
```

## The traps, per distribution

| Case | Where it bites | Handling |
|---|---|---|
| **No `pip` at all** | Ubuntu/Debian: `python3` ships without `ensurepip`, `python3 -m venv` dies on *"ensurepip is not available"* | The `venv_capable()` probe (creates a throwaway venv **and** checks `pip` answers in it), then installs `python3-venv python3-pip` |
| **`pip` in a separate package** | Alpine (`py3-pip`), Arch (`python-pip`), Fedora/openSUSE/Void (`python3-pip`) | The same probe, package name per family (`pkg_names pipvenv`) |
| **An "externally managed" environment (PEP 668)** | Debian ≥12, Ubuntu ≥23.04, Fedora ≥38, Arch, openSUSE, Homebrew: `pip install` refuses to touch the system | We **never** install into the system: everything goes through the venv. No `--break-system-packages` |
| **`python3-venv` points at the wrong interpreter** | Debian/Ubuntu with several Pythons (deadsnakes) | Falls back to `python<X.Y>-venv`, then `python3-full` |
| **`venv` definitively unavailable** | Trimmed-down Python images | Falls back to `virtualenv` (which brings its own pip), the distro package then the binary |
| **No Python ≥ 3.10** | Minimal Debian, old RHEL | Installs the family's `python` package, then looks again (newest to oldest) |
| **No `sudo`** | Containers (often root), Termux | root → an empty prefix; neither root nor `sudo` → an explicit message, we do not try |
| **`sudo` with no password possible** | A non-interactive job | Detected (`sudo -n` + no TTY): the command to run by hand is printed instead of blocking on a prompt |
| **Interactive `apt`** | Debian/Ubuntu | `DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive` |
| **A partial upgrade** | Arch (`pacman -Sy` then install = a broken system) | `pacman -Syu --needed` before any install |
| **A package name this release does not know** | `ninja` vs `ninja-build`, `python3-dev` vs `python3-devel` | The batch is retried **package by package**: one unknown name does not cancel the others |
| **`cmake` too old** | CentOS 7 and friends (2.8) — liboqs will not configure | The version is checked (≥ 3.18), otherwise `pip install cmake ninja` inside the venv |
| **An unknown distro** | Exotic derivatives | `ID`/`ID_LIKE` first, otherwise the package manager actually installed; as a last resort cmake/ninja through pip |
| **Declarative distros** | NixOS, Gentoo | We install nothing behind the user's back: a `nix-shell` / `emerge` command is printed |
| **No wheel for the platform** | musl, 32-bit ARM, RISC-V: `cryptography` compiles | On a `pip install` failure, installs Rust + the OpenSSL/FFI headers (`pkg_names native`) and retries |
| **No `git`** | Minimal images | The liboqs sources are fetched as a tarball (same ref, same origin) with `curl` |
| **An inherited broken venv** | A distro upgrade that removes the interpreter, a venv created without pip | `venv_broken()` detects it on every run and recreates it |
| **Little RAM, many cores** | The liboqs build gets OOM-killed (~1.5 GB per compilation unit) | Parallelism capped by `MemAvailable` |

## Families handled

`apt` (Debian, Ubuntu, Mint, Pop!\_OS, Kali, Raspbian…), `dnf`/`yum` (Fedora,
RHEL, Rocky, Alma, Amazon Linux, CentOS 7), `pacman` (Arch, Manjaro,
EndeavourOS…), `zypper` (openSUSE, SLES), `apk` (Alpine), `xbps` (Void),
Homebrew (macOS), `pkg` (FreeBSD), `pkg` (Termux/Android), plus the declarative
NixOS and Gentoo cases (instructions only).

## The script's structure

Everything before the `MAIN` banner is nothing but **definitions**; what follows
is the sequence (Python → venv/pip → build tools → venv → dependencies →
verification → launch). The test suite sources the file with
`NMESH_START_LIB=1`: it exercises detection, package names, `sudo`, the venv
probe and the `cmake` check with an isolated `PATH` and fake package managers,
without ever installing anything (`tests/test_start_script.py`).

## Why liboqs is compiled

No distribution ships a liboqs worth trusting: Debian/Ubuntu never had one
(removed from unstable in April 2025), Fedora sits on 0.10.0 — too old to
guarantee ML-KEM-768 / ML-DSA-65. The script therefore compiles the **release
matching exactly the `liboqs-python` wrapper installed**, into
`$OQS_INSTALL_PATH` (default `~/_oqs`), the only place the wrapper looks. On
macOS the official Homebrew formula is accepted after both required algorithms
are checked. The liboqs traps in detail:
[`../Architecture/gotchas.md`](../Architecture/gotchas.md).

### `dht_max_mb` — what this node caches for the network

The DHT store holds content-addressed values other nodes asked us to keep: app
package chunks, release content. It is filled **entirely by what peers store**,
so it is memory this node gives away, and the right number depends on the
machine rather than on the protocol — a node on a LoRa link and a node on a
server have no business sharing one.

Default 32 MiB, settable in `nmesh.conf` between 1 and 4096. It was 128 MiB and
not settable at all, which is a server's number in a project whose point is
running over BLE, LoRa and a USB stick. Eviction is least-recently-used, so a
smaller store does not fail — it forgets sooner.

### `abuse_*` — how quick this node is to stop serving a peer

Each application judges by its own thresholds — only chat knows what too many
messages is — and reports the sender. These three say what those reports add up
to, and like `dht_max_mb` they are a property of the deployment rather than of
the protocol: a node on a hostile link and a node among machines its operator
installed themselves want different numbers.

| Setting | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `abuse_suspect` | 8 | Score at which a peer stops being served: its traffic is dropped, it is told nothing, and the link is let go a little later |
| `abuse_hostile` | 20 | Score at which it is also refused a fresh connection |
| `abuse_halflife` | 3600 | Seconds for a complaint to lose half its weight, so a node that misbehaved once and then behaved comes back on its own |
| `no_abuse_gossip` | off | Neither send nor act on other nodes' reports — judge only what this node saw itself |

### `mlo_*` and `keepalive_*` — spending two links instead of one

A node often holds two links to the same peer (a LAN address and a punched UDP
path, IPv4 and IPv6) and until now exactly one of them carried anything.
Multi-link operation spends both, and its whole cost is a probe every hundred
milliseconds on each bundled link — which is cheap on Ethernet and expensive on
a battery, so nothing here is on by default.

Which **media** may be bundled is *not* set here: it is a checkbox per transport
(`tcp.mlo`, `udp.mlo`, under Network → Reachability, or `tcp.mlo = true` in this
file), because only the operator knows what a probe ten times a second costs on
that medium. A transport that does not offer the option at all — the spool, a
USB stick carried on foot — can never be bundled.

What is set here is everything that is not the medium's business:

| Setting | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `mlo_always` | off | Keep bundling even when nothing is using this node. Off, it wakes with the console or an attached app and sleeps again a couple of minutes after the last of them goes |
| `mlo_skew_ms` | 30 | How far apart two links may measure and still be bundled. Further apart, striping delivers half the packets late, which every consumer reads as loss |
| `mlo_drop_percent` | 10 | Share of its last 50 probes a bundled link may lose before it is benched. It keeps its probes — that is how it measures its way back — and rejoins at *half* this, because one number in both directions is a link that flaps in and out on a single probe |
| `keepalive_fast_min_ms` | 100 | The fastest this node will ever be probed, striping or not |
| `keepalive_fast_max_ms` | 1000 | The slowest cadence this node still calls *fast*. A peer whose fast range does not reach it gets **no striping** — which is the strongest opt-out here, and the one a battery node wants |
| `keepalive_slow_min_ms` | 15000 | The fastest this node wants to be probed when nothing is happening |
| `keepalive_slow_max_ms` | 20000 | The slowest before this node stops believing the link. Kept under the medium's own timeout whatever is agreed, so a deep sleep means raising `tcp.read_timeout` too |

These four are a range **per mode**, and two nodes take the fastest floor both
offered — in each mode. Raising a floor here is therefore an opt-out no peer can
talk back down, and nothing a peer sends lowers either of this node's cadences:
that is the shape of the arithmetic rather than a limit bolted onto it, which is
why there are four numbers and not two. The mechanics, the reason the
two-number version was unsafe, and the three behavioural findings that come out
of it are in
[`../Architecture/transports.md`](../Architecture/transports.md).

**One thing to know before switching it on:** packets spread over two links
arrive **out of order**, by roughly twice the measured skew. Every built-in app
already copes, and an app on a mesh has never been entitled to assume order
anyway — but a third-party app that does will notice.

### `update_branch` — where the update check looks

Empty by default: the published GitHub releases. Set to a branch name, the check
reads `src/version.py` at that branch instead and *Install* takes its tree. See
[Following a branch instead of the releases](#following-a-branch-instead-of-the-releases).

### `update_check_minutes` / `update_when_active` — when the node goes looking

`update_check_minutes` (5) is how often the node sweeps for an installable
update; **0 means it never goes looking**. `update_when_active` (off) holds that
sweep back while nobody is using the node — no console open, no app attached, no
call.

Neither touches what *arrives*: an announcement from the mesh still wakes the
node, so an operator who publishes an update and is watching for it to land does
not have to wait for anything. What these control is the node going to look on
its own, which on a phone is where the battery goes. An hourly sweep that skips
while the screen is off is a different machine from one checking every five
minutes in a pocket:

```
update_check_minutes = 60
update_when_active = true
```

### `recommend_version` — saying which version this node runs

Off by default. On, the node publishes a signed record pointing at the release
id it runs, carrying the **source digest of the tree actually running here**.

It publishes no code and authorises nothing. What it is worth is corroboration:
a second party saying "this code is what I am executing" is the one thing in a
supply chain an attacker cannot obtain by minting identities. Whoever reads it
decides what their own subscriptions make of it — see
[`../Updates/guide`](../Updates/guide).

One report is worth at most 4, so it always takes several to reach either
threshold. What the numbers mean, and why an accusation from a stranger counts
for nothing: [`../Architecture/security.md`](../Architecture/security.md).
